# The todos round trip: type, add, walk the add back and forward
# through the Edit menu, then toggle the stamped row's checkbox (a
# field-level update). Every reading is of ONE label — the derived
# "items left" signal — because the whole scene is about who keeps it
# correct.
#
# WHAT THE UNDO PART PINS. A derived signal is recomputed by the binding
# after every mutation and written INTO THAT SAME TRANSACTION, so when
# the transaction is a named step the core banks the derived value in
# both directions of it. Undo restores the label together with the
# collection it derives from, and no binding recomputes anything on the
# way back: absorb_undo folds the payload and nothing else. The two
# expectations under the menu activations are that fact observed on
# every platform instead of reasoned about — docs/deferred.md carries
# the retracted "a derived signal goes stale after an undo" defect, and
# these lines are what replaced it.
#
# THE ADD IS ONE STEP AND FINISHING THE FORM IS NOT. The guest names the
# insert transaction and leaves clear+focus in a second one: `clear`
# inside a group is refused at apply (D4), and an undo that put "buy
# milk" back in the field beside a todo that is gone would be a state
# that never existed (docs/undo-plan.md §2).
#
# AND THE APP WRITES NO UNDO CODE. No todos guest registers on_undone in
# any language, and none needs to — the label is core state, so the core
# restores it. The whole undo surface this scene asks a guest for is the
# Edit menu and one `undoable` call.
#
# TEXT ARRIVES BY set_text HERE, deliberately, where the undo scene
# insists on real keystrokes: a programmatic write leaves nothing on the
# field's own native undo stack (D7), so the Edit>Undo below has exactly
# one possible answer, the ledger's group. Routing is the undo scene's
# subject; this scene wants the group and only the group.
# The opening expect doubles as the scene-ready wait
expect label#0 "0 items left"
set_text entry#0 "buy milk"
click button#0
# THE DERIVE, WRITTEN BY NOBODY'S HANDLER: one todo, not done.
expect label#0 "1 item left"
expect entry#0 ""
expect_focused entry#0

# The step comes back and the label comes back WITH it, out of one
# banked group holding both the entry and the signal the insert dirtied.
# WinUI invokes through the item, and invoking a still-disabled item
# is the stowed-exception crash (docs/traps.md, 0xc000027b). The
# enablement expect is the synchronization, not just the assertion —
# same pacing as undo.steps:65.
expect_menu "Edit>Undo" enabled
menu_activate "Edit>Undo"
expect label#0 "0 items left"

# And forward again. A binding that recomputed the derive on the way
# past instead of letting the core restore it would agree here by
# accident; what this pair catches is a step that banked the collection
# and not the signal, which still reads "1 item left" after the undo and
# never moves again.
expect_menu "Edit>Redo" enabled
menu_activate "Edit>Redo"
expect label#0 "1 item left"

# The restored row is an ordinary row: its key round-tripped through the
# stamped path, so one field's delta still finds it — and the derive is
# still wired to the collection after a walk through the history.
toggle checkbox#0 on
expect label#0 "0 items left"
